course: everyday
lesson: 237
title: Plants that sting, scratch and poison
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most plants are harmless, but a few can sting, burn or poison you. Stinging nettles have tiny hairs that inject a stinging liquid. Poison ivy and poison oak in North America have an oil that gives an itchy, blistering rash: «Leaves of three, let it be».
explain: Giant hogweed is a very tall plant with huge white flower umbrellas, often beside rivers. Its sap makes skin burn badly in sunlight, so never touch it.
explain: Many berries and mushrooms look alike, and some are deadly. Even experts make mistakes with mushrooms, so children never pick or eat wild ones, and eat only berries a grown-up has identified.
explain: If someone has eaten an unknown plant, tell a grown-up at once and keep a piece of the plant, so the poison control centre or doctor knows what it was.
explain: Example: a girl in Ohio gathers sticks for a campfire and spots a vine with three shiny leaves. She leaves it alone, and tells her dad never to burn it, because the smoke would carry the oil into their lungs.
fact: Tiny hairs on stinging nettle leaves inject a liquid that stings and itches, so keep away and wear long trousers on overgrown paths.
fact: «Leaves of three, let it be» helps you avoid poison ivy and poison oak, whose oil causes an itchy, blistering rash.
fact: If a plant makes your skin red and itchy, wash it with soap and water, try not to scratch, and tell a grown-up.
fact: Never burn poison ivy, because the smoke carries its oil into the lungs.
fact: Giant hogweed is a very tall plant with huge white flower umbrellas; its sap makes skin burn badly in sunlight, so never touch it.
fact: Pick only berries a grown-up has identified, above dog height (low ones may have been weed on by dogs) and away from roads, and wash them, because many berries look alike and some are poisonous.
fact: If a child eats an unknown berry or plant, tell a grown-up at once and keep a sample, so poison control or a doctor can help.
fact: Never pick or eat wild mushrooms, and wash your hands if you touched them, because deadly ones look like edible ones.
fact: Do not put wild plants or flowers in your mouth; foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.
fact: Move carefully past roses and brambles, and pull out thorns with a grown-up's help.
fact: Look at cacti but do not touch them, because their spines go into skin and are hard to get out.
fact: Collect conkers, acorns and pine cones for crafts, but never eat conkers or acorns, because they are poisonous.
exercise: Why do stinging nettles hurt? | Tiny hairs inject a liquid that stings | Their leaves are covered in sharp thorns | They give off a gas that burns your skin | Small insects that live on them bite you
exercise: How can you protect your legs on an overgrown path full of nettles? | Wear long trousers | Wear shorts so the leaves slide off | Walk faster through the nettles | Rub your legs with grass first
exercise: What does «leaves of three, let it be» warn you about? | Poison ivy and poison oak | Clover, which is poisonous to eat | Every plant with green leaves | Trees that may drop branches on you
exercise: What does poison ivy oil do to skin? | It causes an itchy, blistering rash | It turns the skin bright green for a whole day | It makes the skin go numb and cold for hours | Nothing at all, unless you eat the leaves
exercise: Why must you never burn poison ivy on a campfire? | The smoke carries its oil into the lungs | It makes the whole fire go out at once | It burns with a poisonous blue flame that blinds people | The leaves explode in the heat
exercise: You see a plant taller than a grown-up with huge white flower umbrellas by a river. What do you do? | Do not touch it; it may be giant hogweed | Pick one of the big flowers to take home | Use its hollow stem as a telescope to look through | Shake it hard to see the seeds fall
exercise: What makes giant hogweed sap so harmful? | It makes skin burn badly in sunlight | It is sticky and pulls out your hair | It smells so bad it makes you faint | It turns into ice on your skin
exercise: Which blackberries are best to pick on a walk? | Ones a grown-up has identified, high up and away from roads | The lowest ones, because they are the easiest to reach | Any dark berries, since they are all ripe | Berries growing right beside the busy road, where the sun is
exercise: Your little cousin has eaten some red berries from a bush. What do you do? | Tell a grown-up at once and keep a piece of the plant | Wait quietly to see if he feels sick later on | Give him lots of berries to compare | Throw the whole plant away so that nobody else can eat it
exercise: You find mushrooms on the lawn. What is right? | Leave them, and wash your hands if you touched them | Pick the white ones, because white ones are safe | Taste a tiny bit to see if it is bitter | Cook them in a pan, because cooking removes all the poison
exercise: Why should you not suck the stem of a wild flower? | Some, like foxgloves and daffodils, are poisonous | Flowers taste bitter and make you cough | Stems are always full of sand and dirt from the ground | Bees will chase you angrily all the way home if you do
exercise: A thorn from a bramble is stuck in your finger. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up to help pull it out | Leave it in, because it will dissolve | Bite it out with your teeth | Push it deeper so it stops hurting
exercise: Why should you look at a cactus but not touch it? | Its spines go into skin and are hard to get out | It sprays out poisonous milk | Cactus leaves are hot from the sun | Touching it makes the cactus die
exercise: What can you do with conkers and acorns you collect? | Use them for crafts but never eat them | Roast them and eat them, just like chestnuts | Feed them to your dog as a crunchy treat | Crack them open for a quick snack on the walk home
test: A boy in Kent has a red, stinging rash after running through a weedy field in shorts. What probably stung him? | Stinging nettles | Daisies | Long dry grass | Dandelions
test: A friend in Virginia wants to make a necklace from a vine with three shiny leaves on each stem. What do you say? | Let it be; it may be poison ivy | Go ahead, three-leaf plants are always safe | Only touch it if the leaves are red | Wash it first and then it is fine
test: Which is true about wild mushrooms? | Even experts can mistake deadly ones for safe ones | Mushrooms that smell nice are always safe | Mushrooms that grow on lawns are always safe to eat | Cooking for long enough makes any wild mushroom safe
test: Why do grown-ups keep a piece of the plant a child has eaten? | It tells the doctor or poison control what the poison was | It is needed so the child can eat it again | It proves to everyone that the child was being naughty | It can be planted later to grow a new one
test: Why pick blackberries above dog height? | The low ones may have been weed on by dogs | The high ones are always the sweetest | Low berries are a different poisonous kind | Dogs bite people picking low berries
test: Which plant is poisonous to eat? | Foxglove | Apple | Carrot | Strawberry
test: Your dad is clearing a garden in Ohio and wants to burn a pile of vines. Why should he check for poison ivy first? | Its smoke carries the oil into the lungs | It makes the fire spread much faster across the garden | Burning it is against all the fire rules | Its ash kills the grass around the fire for many years
test: A girl in Arizona wants to stroke a fuzzy-looking cactus. What is wise? | Just look; even fuzzy cacti have spines that stick in skin | Stroke it gently in the direction of the spines | Touch it, because fuzzy cacti have no spines at all | Wear thin socks on her hands first
test: plant w 3 shiny leaves on the trail, can i pick it | No, leaves of three, let it be | Yes, three leaves means it's clover | Yes, if you wash your hands | Only the small ones
test: dad wants to burn the poison ivy we pulled up | No, the smoke carries its oil into the lungs | Yes, burning kills it safely | Yes, if he stands back a bit from the smoke | Only on a windy day so the smoke blows away
test: lil sis ate a berry off the bush!! | Tell a grown-up now and keep a berry to show | Wait n see if she's ok first | Give her lots of sweets to take the taste away | Make her eat another one to check
test: found mushrooms in the garden, look like the ones from the shop | Don't eat them, deadly ones look like safe ones | Fry them, cooking makes any mushroom safe to eat | Eat one, if it tastes nice it's fine | Feed one to the dog first n see if he's ok
explain: A boy in Ohio burned poison ivy with garden rubbish; he breathed in the smoke and his throat and lungs were badly hurt.
explain: A girl in England picked foxglove flowers and licked her fingers; she felt sick and dizzy and needed hospital treatment.
fact: Some common garden and wild plants, such as foxglove and yew, are poisonous if eaten.
fact: Burning poison ivy or oak sends the oil into the smoke, which can hurt your lungs.
test: these purple flowers smell nice, can i eat one | No, many flowers are poisonous | Yes, flowers are safe | Yes, if it's pretty n smells nice | Only the petals, they're fine
test: Why should you never burn poison ivy? | The smoke carries the oil into your lungs | The smoke only smells bad, nothing more | Burning makes it grow back stronger | It makes far too much ash for the garden to cope with
test: touched a weird plant, my hand's red and itchy | Wash it with soap and water and tell an adult | Scratch it hard until it stops itching | Rub it off on your face | Ignore it, it's only a rash, it'll go
test: did u hear some guy burnt a pile of poison ivy in his yard and the smoke made him really sick? real lesson? | Never burn poison ivy; its oil gets into smoke and lungs | Burning poison ivy is the safest way to get rid of it for good | Only touching it is a problem, not smoke | Smoke cleans the oil off the plant
test: apparently a kid touched giant hogweed and his whole arm melted off in the sun. what's probably blown up? | Melted off; but its sap really burns skin in sunlight | That hogweed is a tall plant | That hogweed has white flowers | That sun makes the burns worse
test: my mate says he ate conkers once and was fine so they're safe. true? | No, conkers are poisonous; never eat them | Yes, they're just big nuts like chestnuts | Yes, if they're roasted first | Yes, the shiny ones are fine
exam: Identify the purpose of the rhyme «Leaves of three, let it be». | To assist in the avoidance of poison ivy and poison oak | To identify those wild berries which may be eaten safely | To count the petals of daffodils | To recognise those mushrooms that are safe for consumption
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate action where a child has eaten an unknown berry? | An adult is told at once and a sample retained | The child is given a glass of milk and watched closely | The matter is ignored altogether if the berry was small | The child is made to run about so as to aid digestion
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning wild mushrooms. | Deadly kinds resemble edible kinds and none is to be eaten | Mushrooms that smell pleasant are safe | White mushrooms are invariably safe | Cooking renders all mushrooms safe, whatever their kind or colour
exam: Ascertain which of the following plants is poisonous if eaten. | Foxglove | Lettuce | Carrot | Garden pea
